      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1765108737192,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Y3jegDnt_wNByJWdgpEyA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1533.425036859457,
      "y": 70.43359837714075,
      "width": 824.6423705826081,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 526339248,
      "version": 8964,
      "versionNonce": 1725474723,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766229297633,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) 429 status code: Too Many requests, you've sent too many requests to a server\nin a short time, triggering a rate limit to prevent overload or abuse. \n\n2) Where should we place the rate limiter.\n    - Bad Solution: Alongside the server\n    Issue: servers are not aware of the global request count, hence the rate limiting\nwill be off by many folds.\n    - Good Solution: Dedicated service\n    Issues: Latency overhead of service calls, point of failure. Decide for fail\nopen(allow all the requests to go through) or fail closed (reject all the requests,\nessentially taking your API offline). dedicated health checks required and for each\nnew service we need to integrate ratelimiting part.\n    - Great Solution: API Gateway\n    Challenges: limited Context about the request, storing RL state requires\nexternal dependency on in-memory store like redis\n\n3) How do we identify clients.\n    - userId, IP address, API key: In reality we can use a combination of these\nthree.\n    - Per-user limits: \"Alice can make 1000 requests/hour\"\n    - Per-IP limits: \"This IP can make 100 requests/minute\"\n    - Global limits: \"Our API can handle 50,000 requests/second total\"\n    - Endpoint-specific limits: \"The search API is limited to 10 requests/minute, but\nprofile updates are 100/minute\n\n4) Rate Limiting Algorithms:\n    - Fixed Window Counter\n    - Sliding Window Counter\n    - Token Bucket\n\n5) The state about the token bucket needs to be shared across all the intances\nof the gateway. We use redis as central sourrce of truth for all token budcket\nstate. Note that there is going to be dual race conditions that needs to be taken\ncare of.\n    RC1 : Request arrives, reads the current bucket tocken count and accordingly\nupdates it via atomic operation. here the update part is taken care of\n    RC2 : two different requests come at same time and reads same quantity, to\ndeal with this we can make the whole read-calculate-update logic part of a single\natomic transaction.\n\n6) Limit is exceeded what now?\nwe have two options to deal with this.\n    a) fail - fast, simply return 429 along with error headers\n    b) Queue the requests for later processing once the tokens are refilled. : this\napproach is not a good approach for most of the cases. why ? queueing requests\ncan take up alot of memory",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) 429 status code: Too Many requests, you've sent too many requests to a server in a short time, triggering a rate limit to prevent overload or abuse. \n\n2) Where should we place the rate limiter.\n    - Bad Solution: Alongside the server\n    Issue: servers are not aware of the global request count, hence the rate limiting will be off by many folds.\n    - Good Solution: Dedicated service\n    Issues: Latency overhead of service calls, point of failure. Decide for fail open(allow all the requests to go through) or fail closed (reject all the requests, essentially taking your API offline). dedicated health checks required and for each new service we need to integrate ratelimiting part.\n    - Great Solution: API Gateway\n    Challenges: limited Context about the request, storing RL state requires external dependency on in-memory store like redis\n\n3) How do we identify clients.\n    - userId, IP address, API key: In reality we can use a combination of these three.\n    - Per-user limits: \"Alice can make 1000 requests/hour\"\n    - Per-IP limits: \"This IP can make 100 requests/minute\"\n    - Global limits: \"Our API can handle 50,000 requests/second total\"\n    - Endpoint-specific limits: \"The search API is limited to 10 requests/minute, but profile updates are 100/minute\n\n4) Rate Limiting Algorithms:\n    - Fixed Window Counter\n    - Sliding Window Counter\n    - Token Bucket\n\n5) The state about the token bucket needs to be shared across all the intances of the gateway. We use redis as central sourrce of truth for all token budcket state. Note that there is going to be dual race conditions that needs to be taken care of.\n    RC1 : Request arrives, reads the current bucket tocken count and accordingly updates it via atomic operation. here the update part is taken care of\n    RC2 : two different requests come at same time and reads same quantity, to deal with this we can make the whole read-calculate-update logic part of a single atomic transaction.\n\n6) Limit is exceeded what now?\nwe have two options to deal with this.\n    a) fail - fast, simply return 429 along with error headers\n    b) Queue the requests for later processing once the tokens are refilled. : this approach is not a good approach for most of the cases. why ? queueing requests can take up alot of memory",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
