    {
      "id": "HCq5hZtkx_UA7OvUQuhuO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 899.2456707790341,
      "y": -70.27292141765497,
      "width": 985.4800415039062,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1988709987,
      "version": 137,
      "versionNonce": 272502435,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1765856375083,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For this breakdown we are going to design request level rate limiting for social media platform's API ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For this breakdown we are going to design request level rate limiting for social media platform's API ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 725.7553195652615,
      "y": 831.5404342316938,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 389,
      "versionNonce": 756174115,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1766229537632,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Scalability to 1M rps: the current single node redis can take upto 100k\nrps hence we will require around 10 nodes to take care of this. for that we\nwill be doing sharding based on the key. Now inorder to make sure every\ntime user a's request goes to shard A and vice versa we can use sething\nlike consistent hashing. At production lvl this is done via something called\nreddis cluster. -- need to read more about it.\n\n2) Availability & fault tolerance: What happens when one of the shard goes\ndown. all the users specific to that shard gets effected. for such cases we\ncan pick on of the two apprroaches fail - closed or fail -open. But better\nthing would be to ensure we dont come to this. for that we could have\nreplicas for the redis nodes and Inorder to deal with latency issue we can\nuse the async syncing of replicas.\n\n3) latency: While the redis resp time is less, the network call to and fro\nfrom redis would take some time(20-50ms) due to the tcp handshake\noverhead for each request. we can solve this issue by using a connection\npool of persistent connections. another big win would be geographical\ndistribution of the rate limiting infra near to the user.\n\n4) handling hot keys:\n    - legit high volume clients: equest queueing\n    - abusive traffic: DDOS protection, temporary blocking of the ip/API\nkey\n\n5) Taking care of dynamic rule configuration:\n    a) good solution: db polling\n    b) great solution: using push based system where configuration changes\nare immediately sent ot all the api gateways. zookeeper.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Scalability to 1M rps: the current single node redis can take upto 100k rps hence we will require around 10 nodes to take care of this. for that we will be doing sharding based on the key. Now inorder to make sure every time user a's request goes to shard A and vice versa we can use sething like consistent hashing. At production lvl this is done via something called reddis cluster. -- need to read more about it.\n\n2) Availability & fault tolerance: What happens when one of the shard goes down. all the users specific to that shard gets effected. for such cases we can pick on of the two apprroaches fail - closed or fail -open. But better thing would be to ensure we dont come to this. for that we could have replicas for the redis nodes and Inorder to deal with latency issue we can use the async syncing of replicas.\n\n3) latency: While the redis resp time is less, the network call to and fro from redis would take some time(20-50ms) due to the tcp handshake overhead for each request. we can solve this issue by using a connection pool of persistent connections. another big win would be geographical distribution of the rate limiting infra near to the user.\n\n4) handling hot keys:\n    - legit high volume clients: equest queueing\n    - abusive traffic: DDOS protection, temporary blocking of the ip/API key\n\n5) Taking care of dynamic rule configuration:\n    a) good solution: db polling\n    b) great solution: using push based system where configuration changes are immediately sent ot all the api gateways. zookeeper.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
